IMPORT prelude

-- Marine Insurance Act 1906 (Singapore) -- whether a marine insurer must pay, and how much: gaming policies and insurable interest
-- (ss 4, 6), the assured's duty of disclosure (ss 17, 18), warranties and seaworthiness (ss 33, 34, 39), deviation and delay (ss 46,
-- 48, 49), the losses an insurer is and is not liable for (s 55), total loss and notice of abandonment (ss 57, 60 to 62), the
-- measure of indemnity (ss 67 to 69, 71, 81) and return of premium (s 84).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/MIA1906.txt. The deposit says the revised edition
-- "incorporates all amendments up to and including 1 December 2021 and comes into operation on 31 December 2021"; SSO's retrieval
-- record gives it as the version current at 1 October 2026. No amending Act is annotated in the body. The Legislative History says
-- this is the English Act 6 Edw. VII, c. 41 (commenced 1 January 1907), declared by the Application of English Law Act 1993 to apply
-- in Singapore from 12 November 1993, and revised as Chapter 387 in 1994. THE ARRANGEMENT OF SECTIONS AT THE TOP OF THE DEPOSIT IS
-- OUT OF STEP WITH THE BODY from s 22 on (it prints "23. Voyage and time policies", the body's s 25); the body's numbers are used.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement REQ-0039 in ../../../requirements.jsonl: Tier 2 of the remaining Singapore Acts, ordered by everyday-life
-- relevance. It asks what the Act decides for a person or business it applies to; no scenario has asked a sharper question yet.
-- The person it applies to is a shipowner, cargo owner, charterer or trader who buys marine cover, and that person's insurer.
-- SCOPED to the points at which a claim is won or lost. Not encoded: the definitions in ss 1 to 3, the minor insurable interests
-- (ss 7 to 15), insurable value (s 16), misrepresentation (s 20), the form and construction of the policy (ss 22 to 30 and the Schedule's rules), double
-- insurance and contribution (ss 32, 80), the special warranties (ss 35 to 38, 40, 41), commencement of risk and change of voyage (ss
-- 42 to 45, 47), assignment (ss 50, 51), brokers and premium (ss 52 to 54), general average and salvage (ss 64 to 66, 73), freight
-- and liability measures (ss 70, 72, 74 to 78), subrogation (s 79), mutual insurance (s 85) and the Schedule's form of policy.
-- Nearly every rule here gives way to the policy's own terms ("unless the policy otherwise provides"; s 87 lets implied terms be
-- varied by agreement or usage); this encoding assumes a policy that is silent.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules conventions. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Marine Insurance Act 1906 -- claims`

GIVEN a IS A NUMBER, b IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lesser of` a b MEANS IF a AT MOST b THEN a ELSE b

§§ `4, 6 -- gaming policies and insurable interest`

-- s 4(1): "Every contract of marine insurance by way of gaming or wagering is void." (2) It is deemed gaming (a) "where the assured
-- has not an insurable interest ... and the contract is entered into with no expectation of acquiring such an interest"; or (b)
-- where the policy is made "interest or no interest", or "without further proof of interest than the policy itself", or "without
-- benefit of salvage to the insurer" -- the last allowed "where there is no possibility of salvage".
GIVEN `has an insurable interest` IS A BOOLEAN, `expects to acquire one` IS A BOOLEAN, `made interest or no interest` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the policy is void as gaming` IF
        `made interest or no interest`
    OR  ((NOT `has an insurable interest`) AND (NOT `expects to acquire one`))

-- s 6(1): "The assured must be interested in the subject-matter insured at the time of the loss though he need not be interested
-- when the insurance is effected". Proviso: insured "lost or not lost", the assured may recover though the interest came after the
-- loss, "unless at the time of effecting the contract of insurance the assured was aware of the loss, and the insurer was not."
GIVEN `interested at the time of loss` IS A BOOLEAN, `insured lost or not lost` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `assured knew of the loss when contracting` IS A BOOLEAN, `insurer knew of the loss when contracting` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the interest requirement is met` IF
        `interested at the time of loss`
    OR  (`insured lost or not lost`
         AND (NOT (`assured knew of the loss when contracting` AND (NOT `insurer knew of the loss when contracting`))))

§§ `17, 18 -- utmost good faith and disclosure`

-- s 17: "a contract based upon the utmost good faith"; if not observed by either party the contract "may be avoided by the other".
-- s 18(1): the assured must disclose, before the contract is concluded, "every material circumstance which is known to the assured",
-- and is deemed to know what "in the ordinary course of business, ought to be known by him"; if not, "the insurer may avoid the
-- contract". (2) Material = "would influence the judgment of a prudent insurer in fixing the premium or determining whether he will
-- take the risk". (3) "In the absence of inquiry" need not be disclosed: (a) what diminishes the risk; (b) what is known or presumed
-- known to the insurer (matters of common notoriety); (c) what the insurer waives; (d) what is superfluous by reason of a warranty.
DECLARE Circumstance HAS
    `material` IS A BOOLEAN
    `known or ought to be known to the assured` IS A BOOLEAN
    `disclosed` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the insurer asked about it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it diminishes the risk` IS A BOOLEAN
    `known or presumed known to the insurer` IS A BOOLEAN
    `information waived by the insurer` IS A BOOLEAN
    `superfluous because of a warranty` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN c IS A Circumstance
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `it falls in an exception to disclosure` IF
        (NOT c's `the insurer asked about it`)
    AND (    c's `it diminishes the risk`
         OR  c's `known or presumed known to the insurer`
         OR  c's `information waived by the insurer`
         OR  c's `superfluous because of a warranty`)

GIVEN c IS A Circumstance
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the insurer may avoid for non-disclosure` IF
        c's `material`
    AND c's `known or ought to be known to the assured`
    AND (NOT c's `disclosed`)
    AND (NOT `it falls in an exception to disclosure` c)

§§ `33, 34, 39 -- warranties and seaworthiness`

-- s 33(3): a warranty "is a condition which must be exactly complied with, whether it be material to the risk or not"; if not, "the
-- insurer is discharged from liability as from the date of the breach ... but without prejudice to any liability incurred by him
-- before that date". s 34(1): excused when the warranty "ceases to be applicable" by change of circumstances, or compliance is made
-- "unlawful by any subsequent law". (2): the assured "cannot avail himself of the defence that the breach has been remedied ...
-- before loss". (3): "A breach of warranty may be waived by the insurer."
-- Materiality and remedy are carried as fields so that the cases can show they change nothing.
DECLARE WarrantyFacts HAS
    `broken` IS A BOOLEAN
    `material to the risk` IS A BOOLEAN
    `remedied before the loss` IS A BOOLEAN
    `excused by changed circumstances or a later law` IS A BOOLEAN
    `waived by the insurer` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the loss came before the breach` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN w IS A WarrantyFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the insurer is still liable despite the warranty` IF
        (NOT w's `broken`)
    OR  w's `excused by changed circumstances or a later law`
    OR  w's `waived by the insurer`
    OR  w's `the loss came before the breach`

-- s 39(1): "In a voyage policy, there is an implied warranty that, at the commencement of the voyage, the ship shall be seaworthy".
-- (5): "In a time policy, there is no implied warranty that the ship shall be seaworthy at any stage", but where "with the privity of
-- the assured, the ship is sent to sea in an unseaworthy state, the insurer is not liable for any loss attributable to
-- unseaworthiness". A voyage-policy breach discharges the insurer under s 33(3) whether or not the loss was caused by it.
DECLARE PolicyKind IS ONE OF
    `voyage policy`
    `time policy`

GIVEN `the kind` IS A PolicyKind, `unseaworthy when she sailed` IS A BOOLEAN, `with the assured's privity` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `loss attributable to unseaworthiness` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `unseaworthiness defeats the claim` IF
    CONSIDER `the kind`
    WHEN `voyage policy` THEN `unseaworthy when she sailed`
    WHEN `time policy` THEN `unseaworthy when she sailed` AND `with the assured's privity` AND `loss attributable to unseaworthiness`

§§ `46, 48, 49 -- deviation and delay`

-- s 46(1): where a ship "without lawful excuse, deviates from the voyage contemplated by the policy, the insurer is discharged from
-- liability as from the time of deviation, and it is immaterial that the ship may have regained her route before any loss occurs".
-- (3) "The intention to deviate is immaterial; there must be a deviation in fact". s 48: in a voyage policy, unreasonable delay
-- without lawful excuse likewise discharges. s 49(1): excused (a) where authorised by the policy; (b) circumstances beyond the
-- control of the master and his employer; (c) to comply with a warranty; (d) for the safety of the ship or subject-matter; (e) "for
-- the purpose of saving human life, or aiding a ship in distress where human life may be in danger"; (f) to obtain medical or surgical
-- aid for any person on board; (g) barratry of the master or crew, "if barratry be one of the perils insured against".
DECLARE Reason IS ONE OF
    `authorised by the policy`
    `beyond the control of the master and owner`
    `to comply with a warranty`
    `for the safety of the ship`
    `to save human life`
    `to aid a ship in distress with lives at risk`
    `to aid a ship in distress to earn salvage`
    `to get medical aid for someone on board`
    `barratry, which the policy covers`
    `barratry, which the policy does not cover`
    `to load extra cargo`

GIVEN `the reason` IS A Reason
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the deviation is excused` IF
    CONSIDER `the reason`
    WHEN `to aid a ship in distress to earn salvage` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `barratry, which the policy does not cover` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `to load extra cargo` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `deviated in fact` IS A BOOLEAN, `the reason` IS A Reason
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the insurer is discharged by the deviation` IF
    `deviated in fact` AND (NOT `the deviation is excused` `the reason`)

§§ `55 -- included and excluded losses`

-- s 55(1): the insurer "is liable for any loss proximately caused by a peril insured against", and not otherwise. (2)(a) not liable
-- for "any loss attributable to the wilful misconduct of the assured", but liable even though the loss "would not have happened but
-- for the misconduct or negligence of the master or crew"; (b) on ship or goods, not liable for loss proximately caused by delay,
-- "although the delay be caused by a peril insured against"; (c) not liable for "ordinary wear and tear, ordinary leakage and
-- breakage, inherent vice, or nature of the subject-matter insured", "rats or vermin", or injury to machinery not caused by maritime
-- perils. (b) and (c), and the crew limb of (a), apply "unless the policy otherwise provides"; the assured's wilful misconduct does not.
DECLARE Cause IS ONE OF
    `a storm, crew negligence aside`
    `a storm made worse by crew negligence`
    `the assured's wilful misconduct`
    `delay caused by a storm`
    `ordinary wear and tear`
    `inherent vice`
    `rats`

GIVEN `the cause` IS A Cause, `the policy expressly covers it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the insurer is liable for the loss` IF
    CONSIDER `the cause`
    WHEN `a storm, crew negligence aside` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a storm made worse by crew negligence` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the assured's wilful misconduct` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE `the policy expressly covers it`

§§ `57, 60, 61, 62 -- total loss and notice of abandonment`

-- s 57(1): actual total loss where the subject-matter is destroyed, ceases to be a thing of the kind insured, or the assured "is
-- irretrievably deprived thereof"; (2) "no notice of abandonment need be given". s 61: on a constructive total loss the assured may
-- treat it as partial, or abandon and treat it as actual. s 62(1): the assured who abandons "must give notice of abandonment; and if
-- he fails to do so, the loss can only be treated as a partial loss". (7) notice is unnecessary where "there would be no possibility
-- of benefit to the insurer"; (8) "Notice of abandonment may be waived by the insurer."
DECLARE LossKind IS ONE OF
    `actual total loss`
    `constructive total loss`
    `partial loss`

GIVEN `the loss` IS A LossKind, `notice of abandonment given` IS A BOOLEAN, `notice excused or waived` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the loss is paid as a total loss` IF
    CONSIDER `the loss`
    WHEN `actual total loss` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `constructive total loss` THEN `notice of abandonment given` OR `notice excused or waived`
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 60(2)(b): a damaged ship is a constructive total loss where "the cost of repairing the damage would exceed the value of the ship
-- when repaired"; the proviso: no deduction for general average contributions payable by other interests, "but account is to be
-- taken of the expense of future salvage operations". s 27(4): the agreed value in a valued policy "is not conclusive" for this.
GIVEN `cost of repairs` IS A NUMBER, `future salvage` IS A NUMBER, `value when repaired` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the damaged ship is a constructive total loss` IF
    `cost of repairs` PLUS `future salvage` GREATER THAN `value when repaired`

§§ `67, 68, 69, 71, 81 -- the measure of indemnity`

-- s 68: on a total loss, (a) valued policy -- "the sum fixed by the policy"; (b) unvalued -- "the insurable value". s 27(3): the
-- agreed value is "conclusive of the insurable value", absent fraud.
GIVEN `valued` IS A BOOLEAN, `agreed value` IS A NUMBER, `insurable value` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the measure of indemnity for a total loss` MEANS
    IF `valued` THEN `agreed value` ELSE `insurable value`

-- s 67(2): each insurer "is liable for such proportion of the measure of indemnity as the amount of his subscription bears to the value
-- fixed by the policy" (valued) "or to the insurable value" (unvalued). s 81: an assured insured for less "is deemed to be his own
-- insurer in respect of the uninsured balance".
GIVEN `measure` IS A NUMBER, `subscription` IS A NUMBER, `value` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the insurer's share` MEANS `measure` TIMES `subscription` DIVIDED BY `value`

-- s 71(c): goods delivered damaged -- such proportion of the sum fixed (valued) or the insurable value (unvalued) "as the difference
-- between the gross sound and damaged values at the place of arrival bears to the gross sound value".
GIVEN `base` IS A NUMBER, `gross sound value` IS A NUMBER, `gross damaged value` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the measure for goods delivered damaged` MEANS
    `base` TIMES (`gross sound value` MINUS `gross damaged value`) DIVIDED BY `gross sound value`

-- s 69: a ship damaged but not totally lost -- (a) repaired: "the reasonable cost of the repairs, less the customary deductions, but
-- not exceeding the sum insured in respect of any one casualty"; (b) partially repaired: those repairs plus reasonable depreciation,
-- "provided that the aggregate amount shall not exceed the cost of repairing the whole damage"; (c) unrepaired and unsold: "the
-- reasonable depreciation ... but not exceeding the reasonable cost of repairing such damage".
GIVEN `cost of the repairs` IS A NUMBER, `customary deductions` IS A NUMBER, `sum insured` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the measure for a repaired ship` MEANS
    `the lesser of` (`cost of the repairs` MINUS `customary deductions`) `sum insured`

GIVEN `repairs done` IS A NUMBER, `depreciation` IS A NUMBER, `cost to repair everything` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the measure for a partially repaired ship` MEANS
    `the lesser of` (`repairs done` PLUS `depreciation`) `cost to repair everything`

GIVEN `depreciation` IS A NUMBER, `cost to repair` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the measure for an unrepaired ship` MEANS
    `the lesser of` `depreciation` `cost to repair`

§§ `84 -- return of premium`

-- s 84(1): where the consideration "totally fails, and there has been no fraud or illegality on the part of the assured", the
-- premium is returnable. (3)(a) void or avoided from commencement: returnable absent fraud or illegality, "but if the risk is not
-- apportionable, and has once attached, the premium is not returnable"; (b) never imperilled: returnable -- but insured "lost or not
-- lost" and already arrived safely, not returnable "unless, at such time, the insurer knew of the safe arrival"; (c) no insurable
-- interest throughout: returnable, but not for "a policy effected by way of gaming or wagering"; (d) a defeasible interest ended
-- during the risk: "not returnable"; (f) double insurance "effected knowingly by the assured": "no premium is returnable".
DECLARE PremiumEvent IS ONE OF
    `the cargo never sailed`
    `insured lost or not lost, already arrived, insurer unaware`
    `insured lost or not lost, already arrived, insurer knew`
    `no insurable interest at any time during the risk`
    `a gaming policy`
    `a defeasible interest ended during the risk`
    `avoided for the assured's fraud`
    `avoided for innocent non-disclosure before the risk attached`
    `avoided for innocent non-disclosure after an indivisible risk attached`
    `double insurance taken out knowingly`

GIVEN `the event` IS A PremiumEvent
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the premium is returnable` IF
    CONSIDER `the event`
    WHEN `the cargo never sailed` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `insured lost or not lost, already arrived, insurer knew` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `no insurable interest at any time during the risk` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `avoided for innocent non-disclosure before the risk attached` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
